// A program whose entry is `fn build` is a build program however ctxc is asked to build
// it (spec §19, rule 2), so it has build::declarations as a file too. Run that way, its
// graph goes to standard output, and its paths are from the working directory. A program
// with a `main` isn't one, whatever else it declares.
//# $ ctxc run build.ctx
//# cwd: app
//# stdout escaped:
//| exe\t0\tt\tsrc
//| file src/main.ctx
//| struct Main @ src/main.ctx:1:1
//# $ ctxc exe build.ctx -o {dir}/built
//# cwd: app
//# exit: 0
// The interpreter takes it for a build program too: it reports no error in it. (It doesn't
// run it, for the memory it reserves, which isn't this case's subject: only that standard
// error has no `error:` is said.)
//# $ ctxc interp build.ctx
//# cwd: app
//# exit: 1
//# stderr lacks:
//| error:
//# $ ctxc run plain.ctx
//# exit: 1
//# stderr:
//| plain.ctx:1:29: error: no type or namespace `Decl` in `build`
